Investigation Lesson

A TCP connection is established through a three-way handshake before data is exchanged.

Analyzing the SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK packets makes it possible to understand how TCP synchronizes sequence numbers and confirms communication between two endpoints.

I also observed that packet captures can contain unrelated background traffic. Therefore, traffic should be identified carefully based on IP addresses, ports, protocols, and connection context rather than assuming every captured packet belongs to the activity being investigated.

Conclusion

This exercise helped me move from understanding TCP as a networking concept to observing the TCP connection-establishment process at the packet level.

I learned how to identify the TCP three-way handshake and understand the relationship between TCP ports, flags, sequence numbers, and acknowledgment numbers.



